// ---- System_geminiDraft (full HW/SW architecture) ----
{"id": "gemini-dual-core-split", "question": "Hi, could you explain how compute is split across the two ESP32-S3 cores in our bot design? Which core runs YOLO and which handles navigation and sensors?", "expected": "Core1 (APP_CPU) runs only YOLO/TFLite-Micro inference on frames from a PSRAM buffer taking hundreds of ms, pinned with xTaskCreatePinnedToCore, signalling Core0 via FreeRTOS queue/task notification. Core0 (PRO_CPU) handles ESP-IDF WiFi/networking, navigation, sensor polling, sensor fusion (x,y,theta), SD/camera, and PID motor control.", "expectedSources": ["System_geminiDraft"], "tags": ["answerable", "architecture"]}
{"id": "gemini-nav-layers", "question": "How do the three navigation layers (reactive, planning, execution) work together, and what are the Rr/Rob zones and the 15cm rule?", "expected": "L1 Reactive is highest-priority veto reflex: HC-SR04 ultrasonic, else vision danger zone (large box in bottom half of frame), threshold e.g. 15cm triggers STOP/REVERSE. L2 Planning is a Potential Field: waypoint attraction proportional to distance plus YOLO obstacle repulsion with Rr (no force outside, ramp inside) and Rob (max threat); net vector gives heading+speed with waypoint following and dynamic replanning. L3 Execution is PID comparing desired vs actual pose from encoders plus magnetometer, driving left/right PWM.", "expectedSources": ["System_geminiDraft"], "tags": ["answerable", "navigation"]}
{"id": "gemini-hardware-bill", "question": "What is the full hardware bill for the bot per the system draft — MCU, camera, sensors, storage, and motor drivers?", "expected": "ESP32-S3-N16R8 (16MB flash for program and maps, 8MB PSRAM for image and model), OV7670 camera over parallel I2S, QMC5883L magnetometer over I2C for absolute north to fix encoder drift, Hall/optical wheel encoders on GPIO for dead reckoning, microSD over SPI for tile maps and logs, DRV8833/L298N motor drivers with GPIO PWM speed and digital direction.", "expectedSources": ["System_geminiDraft"], "tags": ["answerable", "hardware"]}
{"id": "gemini-mapping-localization", "question": "How does mapping and localization work — tile maps, dead reckoning drift, and ArUco markers?", "expected": "Tile maps stored on SD (e.g. 10x10cm tiles labelled Unexplored/Free_Space/Static_Obstacle/Waypoint). Dead reckoning from encoders is fast but drifts; the magnetometer corrects theta; ArUco markers at known map positions hard-reset x,y. Scenario: load map plus waypoints A and End from SD, start at an ArUco marker, attract to A, arc around a YOLO-detected chair using distance/direction, then switch target to End within radius of A.", "expectedSources": ["System_geminiDraft"], "tags": ["answerable", "mapping"]}

// ---- System_gptDraft (alternative spec with formulas) ----
{"id": "gpt-potential-math", "question": "Could you share the potential-field equations from the system spec and explain Rs, Rob, Rr, Kt and Ko?", "expected": "Fatt = Kt*(pgoal - probot); Frep = Ko*(1/d - 1/Rob)*(probot - pobs)/d^3 if d <= Rob else 0. Rs is the gravitational attenuation zone range, Rob the obstacle threat range, Rr the repulsion transition zone range. Kt/Ko are attraction/repulsion gains to tune. Net force maps to heading and speed; next steps are choosing tile resolution, tuning Kt/Ko, benchmarking inference vs framerate with frame-skipping, and keeping ultrasonic as last-ditch stop.", "expectedSources": ["System_gptDraft"], "tags": ["answerable", "navigation", "math"]}
{"id": "gpt-tiles-fusion-fallback", "question": "What tile size, sensor-fusion inputs, and A-star fallback logic does the GPT system draft specify?", "expected": "Tiles e.g. 0.5m x 0.5m, sparse on SD and loaded on demand, marked with YOLO obstacles, sequenced center-to-center, with a local KxK A* if blocked. Fusion is EKF/complementary: magnetometer heading plus odometry/optical-flow deltas plus ArUco fixes. Perception: YOLO-TFLite-Micro boxes, depth via stereo/mono-CNN/bbox scaling, Lucas-Kanade/Farneback optical flow downsampled, ArUco 6DOF line-of-sight. Global A*/D* runs only when a waypoint is unreachable or reactive control fails. Camera runs 5-10 FPS downscaled.", "expectedSources": ["System_gptDraft"], "tags": ["answerable", "mapping"]}
{"id": "gpt-core-assignment", "question": "In the GPT draft, which core does what? I heard it differs from the Gemini draft.", "expected": "Yes, it is swapped vs the Gemini draft: Core0 handles real-time sensors and motors, Core1 handles TFLite YOLO vision plus planning. Hardware diagram: power to ESP32 dual-core Xtensa LX6 with WiFi/BLE and SPI/I2C/UART to IMU magnetometer/compass, encoders, motors/motor controller, Hall current sensors, OV7670 camera, and microSD map storage.", "expectedSources": ["System_gptDraft"], "tags": ["answerable", "architecture"]}

// ---- YOLO Arduino IDE (Arduino guide) ----
{"id": "arduino-toolchain-setup", "question": "Hi! What are the exact Arduino IDE setup steps for YOLO on the ESP32-S3 — board URL, board selection, and TFLite library?", "expected": "Add https://raw.githubusercontent.com/espressif/arduino-esp32/master/package_esp32_index.json to Additional Boards URLs, install esp32 by Espressif via Boards Manager, select ESP32S3 Dev Module with PSRAM enabled and 16MB flash. Install the TFLite library from github.com/tensorflow/tflite-micro-arduino or Library Manager, manually copying tensorflow_lite into ~/Documents/Arduino/libraries.", "expectedSources": ["YOLO Arduino IDE"], "tags": ["answerable", "setup", "code"]}
{"id": "arduino-model-export", "question": "How do we export the YOLOv5 Nano model for the S3 (image size, quantization, size limit) and embed it in the sketch?", "expected": "pip install tensorflow==2.13.0 torch torchvision ultralytics; git clone ultralytics/yolov5; pip install -r requirements.txt; python export.py --weights yolov5n.pt --img 96 --include tflite --int8 to get yolov5n-int8.tflite, which must be under ~100KB. Optionally simplify models/yolov5n.yaml (halve filters) and retrain 10 epochs on coco128.yaml then re-export. Embed with xxd -i yolov5n-int8.tflite > model.h (Windows: WSL plus sed replacing unsigned char with static const uint8_t), rename array to yolov5n_model, keep it const for flash.", "expectedSources": ["YOLO Arduino IDE"], "tags": ["answerable", "model"]}
{"id": "arduino-sketch-pins", "question": "What camera pins, tensor arena size, and camera settings does the Arduino sketch use, and what frame rate should I expect?", "expected": "OV2640 pins PWDN 32, RESET -1, XCLK 0, SIOD 26, SIOC 27, Y9 35, Y8 34, Y7 39, Y6 36, Y5 21, Y4 19, Y3 18, Y2 5, VSYNC 25, HREF 23, PCLK 22; xclk 20MHz, RGB565, FRAMESIZE_96X96, jpeg quality 10, fb_count 1. Tensor arena 100*1024 (shrink to 50KB on OOM). Loop: esp_camera_fb_get, normalize buffer into input->data.uint8, Invoke, dump output->data.uint8, apply_nms placeholder. Upload then Serial Monitor at 115200; expect 1-5 seconds per frame.", "expectedSources": ["YOLO Arduino IDE"], "tags": ["answerable", "code", "hardware"]}

// ---- YOLO on ESP32-S3 (ESP-IDF guide) ----
{"id": "espidf-setup-build", "question": "What are the ESP-IDF setup, model conversion, and build/flash steps for YOLO on the S3?", "expected": "Ubuntu: mkdir -p ~/esp; git clone --recursive esp-idf; ./install.sh; . ./export.sh (docs at docs.espressif.com), VSCode plus ESP-IDF extension. Convert: git clone yolov5, pip install -r requirements.txt, export saved_model via export.py --weights yolov5n.pt --include saved_model, then tf.lite.TFLiteConverter.from_saved_model with Optimize.DEFAULT to yolov5n.tflite (optionally shrink imgsz 640 to 96 in models/yolov5n.yaml and prune filters). Embed via xxd -i > model.h. Project: idf.py create-project yolov5_esp32s3; menuconfig set S3 target; copy tensorflow/lite/micro into components. Build: idf.py build; idf.py -p /dev/ttyUSB0 flash; idf.py -p /dev/ttyUSB0 monitor. Keep model under 100KB; expect a few seconds per frame.", "expectedSources": ["YOLO on ESP32-S3"], "tags": ["answerable", "setup", "code"]}
{"id": "espidf-vs-arduino", "question": "How does the ESP-IDF main.c inference flow differ from the Arduino sketch?", "expected": "Same YOLOv5 Nano plus OV2640 target but ESP-IDF C code: kTensorArenaSize 100*1024, MicroErrorReporter, GetModel plus TFLITE_SCHEMA_VERSION check, AllOpsResolver, MicroInterpreter, AllocateTensors, fill input->data.uint8 with camera data, Invoke, then NMS/bbox postprocess looping over output->bytes. Camera via esp-idf/examples/peripherals/camera, capture resized to 96x96 normalized 0-255 to 0-1, static allocation test at 50KB.", "expectedSources": ["YOLO on ESP32-S3", "YOLO Arduino IDE"], "tags": ["answerable", "code", "comparison"]}

