{"id": "papers-adopt-stack", "question": "Which hardware/software stack from the reviewed papers should we reuse for the build?", "expected": "ESP32 as the brain, MPU6050 for yaw/heading, L293D/L298N H-bridge DC motor control, servo for steering, 3D-printed PLA chassis with screwless custom-fit slots, ROS over WiFi to a central PC for development/debug/visualization, and PlatformIO in VSCode for C++ firmware.", "expectedSources": ["Papers Review n  MTHDS"], "tags": ["answerable", "hardware"]}
{"id": "papers-five-gaps", "question": "What are the 5 shortcomings of the reviewed ESP32 papers, and how does our ESP32-S3 design fix each one?", "expected": "P1 no onboard intelligence (papers: server-client ROS with PC processing, ESP32 just executes; ours: fully onboard YOLO plus Potential Field plus hierarchical control = cognitive system). P2 no closed loop (papers: open-loop, control as future work; ours: Movement Layer with wheel-encoder odometry plus magnetometer heading for distance/angle correction). P3 no perception (papers: ESP32-CAM with no vision processing; ours: YOLO via TFLite Micro). P4 rudimentary nav (papers: human/script from ROS master; ours: Hierarchical Reactive/Planning/Execution plus lightweight Potential Field dynamic avoidance). P5 no safety (papers: no ultrasonic/IR, vision-only latency; ours: Reactive Layer reflex with dedicated distance sensors vetoing planning).", "expectedSources": ["Papers Review n  MTHDS"], "tags": ["answerable", "reasoning", "comparison"]}

// ---- Navigation Doc (potential-field-navigation library) ----
{"id": "navdoc-quickstart", "question": "How do I install the potential-field-navigation library and run a first path on a 5x5 grid?", "expected": "pip install potential-field-navigation; from navigation import NavigationSystem, Obstacle. Map is a 2D matrix with -1 = wall/obstacle and 0 = free, plus a safe_radius buffer. Example: 5x5 map with a row [0,-1,-1,-1,0], goal (4,4), safe_radius 1.2, nav.generate_initial_path(), current_pos (1,0), visible [Obstacle((2,2), 1.0)], move_vector = nav.update(...), nav.center(0,0).", "expectedSources": ["Navigation Doc"], "tags": ["answerable", "code", "setup"]}
{"id": "navdoc-api", "question": "What do generate_initial_path, update, and center do, and what map encoding and classes do they expect?", "expected": "NavigationSystem(matrix, goal, safe_radius, potential) with generate_initial_path() following the negative gradient from start via center() to the goal; update(current_pos, visible_obstacles) -> (dx,dy) incrementally recomputes the local field around visible obstacles; center(x,y). PotentialField(attractive_gain, repulsive_gain, attraction(p), repulsion(p,obstacles), compute_field(matrix,goal,obstacles)); Obstacle(position, radius). Concept: attraction to goal plus repulsion from obstacles. Flows documented with classDiagram, flowchart (load map and goal, compute global potential, obstacles ? repulsion : attraction, total potential, gradient descent, path) and a sequence diagram for Bot -> Nav update.", "expectedSources": ["Navigation Doc"], "tags": ["answerable", "code"]}

// ---- Questionaries (viva prep) ----
{"id": "viva-defense-latency", "question": "How do I defend the 5-second inference time, 96x96 resolution, and odometry drift in the viva?", "expected": "5-second inference is useless for a moving vehicle — agree, frame it as a proof of concept finding; need ESP-DL acceleration or FOMO. 96x96 is too small, noisy and unreliable, needing high confidence. Odometry drift of ±13cm may miss a doorway or hit a wall; Stop-and-Think only stops blind driving, better encoders needed. D* Lite replanning is broken by 5-second perception so currently theoretical/slow, with Reactive Priority 1 handling immediate threats. Ghost/false positives (e.g. shadow as wall) handled by 0.5 confidence threshold plus ideally temporal multi-frame consistency.", "expectedSources": ["Questionaries"], "tags": ["answerable", "reasoning"]}
{"id": "viva-arena-nms-fomo", "question": "For the viva: what are Tensor Arena, NMS/IoU for the 567 boxes, and the FOMO vs YOLO trade-off?", "expected": "Tensor Arena (200KB) is pre-allocated RAM for input/output/intermediate tensors. Post-processing takes the 567 boxes to NMS via IoU. Quantization is int8/float16 with accuracy loss (TFLite int8 for MCU). YOLOv5n chosen over MobileNet-SSD/FOMO as the industry single-shot standard to test S3 limits. FOMO is a presence/segmentation detector, not bounding-box, ~30x faster but less spatial. $50 upgrade: Coral USB accelerator or optical quadrature encoders. Tips: frame slowness as a finding, highlight the full stack (web server, websocket, motor, vision, TFLite), know x,y,theta differential-drive math.", "expectedSources": ["Questionaries"], "tags": ["answerable", "reasoning"]}
{"id": "viva-arch-choices", "question": "Why did we pick the ESP32-S3 over a Pi/Jetson Nano, and why the HTTP GET Python server instead of driving the ESP32-CAM directly?", "expected": "ESP32-S3 chosen over Pi/Jetson Nano for cost, power, and TinyML edge AI. HTTP GET Python server (Python server to image bytes to ESP32) chosen to test against a dataset, vs the DevKit limit plus latency of ESP32-CAM direct. Architecture answers in the viva bank.", "expectedSources": ["Questionaries"], "tags": ["answerable", "reasoning"]}

// ---- IEEE Active Learning by Learning (webinar) ----
{"id": "albl-core-idea", "question": "Who presented Active Learning by Learning, and what is the core bandit plus weighted-accuracy idea?", "expected": "Prof. Hsuan-Tien Lin (National Taiwan University, Chief Data Science Consultant at Appier, Learning from Data textbook/MOOC), joint work with Wei-Ning Hsu, AAAI 2015, via IEEE CIS. Active Learning = Learning by Asking: start with small labelled Dl plus large unlabelled Du, iteratively pick x~s from Du, query an oracle/human for y~s, add to Dl, retrain g. ALBL reduces strategy choice to a multi-armed bandit: K strategies A1..AK as arms, EXP4.P picks Ak per iteration suggesting x~t; reward is Weighted Training Accuracy using 1/p_tau importance weights from EXP4.P sampling probabilities as an unbiased estimate of test error (ideal test accuracy is infeasible; naive training accuracy on Dl is biased toward easy points).", "expectedSources": ["IEEE Active Learning by Learning"], "tags": ["answerable", "reasoning"]}
{"id": "albl-libact-limits", "question": "What is libact, and what limitations of ALBL were noted in the talk?", "expected": "libact is the NTU Python active-learning package (Yang 2017, github.com/ntucllab/libact, 500+ stars) implementing ALBL. Limits: not strictly unbiased since g(t) depends on prior queries and the reward is not fixed before the arm pull (wild/non-rigorous bandit use); libact install pain with C dependencies on Windows/Mac; Chembl industry case where uncertainty sampling beats ALBL if the portfolio has a poor arm. Future: transfer learning for AL, NLP/RL, costs, mini-batches, multi-label, weak labels. Lessons: scalability bottleneck is method/parameter choice; heuristic non-rigorous math can work and inspire theory; design-easy does not equal deployment-easy.", "expectedSources": ["IEEE Active Learning by Learning"], "tags": ["answerable", "reasoning"]}
{"id": "albl-strategies", "question": "How does active learning differ from batch duck-feeding, and what are the three human strategies with the no-winner result?", "expected": "Batch ML is duck feeding: algorithm A on labelled set Dl gives hypothesis g approximating target f, but labelling is expensive (e.g. apple classifier, medical images needing expert doctors). Active learning iteratively queries an oracle for the most useful labels to reach high accuracy with far fewer labels. Three strategies: Uncertainty Sampling (most confused), Representative Sampling (dense cluster), Expected Error Reduction. No single winner: uncertainty best on vehicle, PSDS on sonar, QUIRE on diabetes — motivating ALBL, which matches or beats the best single strategy and beats COMB (Baram et al. 2004 balancedness reward).", "expectedSources": ["IEEE Active Learning by Learning"], "tags": ["answerable", "reasoning"]}

